<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
 <section xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-instancing-creating-instances">
    <title>Creating instances</title>
    <introduction>
    <note>
      <title>Note</title>
      <p>This tutorial refers to instancing scenes in the editor. To learn how
                to instance scenes from code, see <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/nodes_and_scene_instances#doc-nodes-and-scene-instances">Nodes and scene instances</url>.</p>
      <p>Godot's approach to <em>instancing</em> described below should not be confused with
                hardware instancing that can be used to render large amounts of similar
                objects quickly. See <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/performance/using_multimesh#doc-using-multimesh">Optimization using MultiMeshes</url> instead.</p>
      </note>
    <p>In the previous part, we saw that a scene is a collection of nodes organized in
            a tree structure, with a single node as its root. You can split your project
            into any number of scenes. This feature helps you break down and organize your
            game's different components.</p>
    <p>You can create as many scenes as you'd like and save them as files with the
            <c>.tscn</c> extension, which stands for "text scene". The <c>label.tscn</c> file from
            the previous lesson was an example. We call those files "Packed Scenes" as they
            pack information about your scene's content.</p>
    <p>Here's an example of a ball. It's composed of a <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_rigidbody2d.html#class-rigidbody2d">RigidBody2D</url> node as its root named Ball, which allows the ball to fall
            and bounce on walls, a <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_sprite2d.html#class-sprite2d">Sprite2D</url> node, and a
            <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_collisionshape2d.html#class-collisionshape2d">CollisionShape2D</url>.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Ball Example</caption>
        <image source="instancing_ball_scene.webp" width="50%" >
         <shortdescription>The Ball Scene is shown with Sprite2D and CollisionShape2D children nodes.</shortdescription>
     </image>
      </figure>
    <p>Once you have saved a scene, it works as a blueprint: you can reproduce it in other
            scenes as many times as you'd like. Replicating an object from a template like
            this is called <term>instancing</term>.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Ball Intances Example</caption>
        <image source="instancing_ball_instances_example.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>The main scene is shown with 6 Ball instances.</shortdescription>
     </image>
      </figure>
    <p>As we mentioned in the previous part, instanced scenes behave like a node: the
            editor hides their content by default. When you instance the Ball, you only see
            the Ball node. Notice also how each duplicate has a unique name.</p>
    <p>Every instance of the Ball scene starts with the same structure and properties
            as <c>ball.tscn</c>. However, you can modify each independently, such as changing
            how they bounce, how heavy they are, or any property exposed by the source
            scene.</p>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-instancing-in-practice">
      <title>In practice</title>
      <p>Let's use instancing in practice to see how it works in Godot.</p>
      <note xml:id="gs-instancing-practice-code-download">
      <title>Download</title>
    <p> We invite
                you to download the instancing sample project we prepared for you:
                <url href="https://github.com/godotengine/godot-docs-project-starters/releases/download/latest-4.x/instancing_starter.zip">instancing_starter.zip</url>.</p>
    </note>

      <activity xml:id="getting-started-instancing_activity" label="getting-started-instancing_activity">
            <title>Changing the Bounce Property Programmatically</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                Before writing any code of your own, run the code, and click on the right size
                where the reference code is running. You'll see the ball drop from where you click.
                Then you can use the up arrow to change the bounce property. Then you can click again.
                Notice the balls start bouncing more the more you press the up arrow. You can also
                press the down arrow to reduce the amount of bouncing. Notice also, that when you click
                on the left side for your code, you'll just see the location of where you clicked.
                Your goal is for your code to act the same way as the reference code.
            </p>
                <p> This activity has multiple parts. For the first part look at the <c>Main.gd</c>
                    file from the <c>instancing_starter</c> code you downloaded. You can copy
                    the code as is, or you can use the <c>clicked</c> variable that is already
                    in the code by making a minor modification.
                </p>

                <p>The second part can be done after reading the next few sections up to
                    <xref ref="getting-started-step-by-step-scripting-player-input-moving-when-pressing-up"/>
                    and then completing the activity after it.
                You'll need to add checks for up and down input to change the bounce value by <c>0.1</c>
                at a time. The maximum bounce value should be <c>0.9</c>, and the minimum should be <c>0.0</c>
                </p>
                <p> The <c>bounce</c> property is on the <c>PhysicsMaterial</c> of the Ball. Once you
              have an instance of a Ball (with type <c>RigidBody2D</c>), you can access it's <c>physics_material_override</c> property and then
              change the <c>bounce</c> property using assignment(<c>=</c>). This will change the bounce for
              all balls in the scene.
            </p>


            <program xml:id="gs-bounce-property" interactive='activecode' language="gdscript" label="gs-bounce-property"
                     pck="gdpractice/L1.P2.creating_instances"
                     scene="res://practices/L1.P2.creating_instances/practice.tscn">

<code>
extends Node

@export var Ball: PackedScene

func _input(event: InputEvent) -> void:
    if event.is_action_pressed("click"):
        var clicked: Vector2 = get_viewport().get_mouse_position()
        print("clicked at: ", clicked)
</code>
<tests>
extends "res://addons/gdpractice/tester/test.gd"
func _init() -> void:
    side_by_side = true
    pass
func _build_checks() -> void:
    var ran_scene := _add_simple_check(
        tr("Ran the scene"),
        func() -> String:
            return ""
    )

</tests>

            </program>
        </statement>
        <hint><p>Are you sure you need a hint? The next hint is only necessary if
                    you are getting an error from the <c>instantiate()</c> method on <c>Ball</c>.
                    The third hint will help you with the <c>if-elif-else</c> structure.
                </p>
                </hint>
                <hint><p>If after adding the ball instantiation code, the program fails but there is
                    no clear error, then you'll need to add the following function to your node:
                </p><program language="gdscript">
func _ready() -> void:
    Ball = load("res://practices/L1.P2.creating_instances/Ball.tscn")
                    </program>
                </hint>
                <hint><p>If you are not sure how to write the decision code here is an example:
                </p><program language="gdscript">
if event.is_action_pressed("click"):
    #do click actions
    pass
elif event.is_action_pressed("ui_up"):
    #do up action (increase bounce)
    pass
elif event.is_action_pressed("ui_down"):
    #do down action (decrease bounce)
    pass
    </program>
                </hint>
                <hint>
                    <p>
                    You can save the first instance of a ball by making a var of
                    type <c>RigidBody2D</c> and setting it to <c>null</c> to start
                    so that you know if you can modify the <c>physics_material_override.bounce</c> property.
                    </p>
                </hint>
        </activity>
      <p>Extract the archive on your computer. To import it, you need the Project Manager.
                The Project Manager is accessed by opening Godot, or if you already have Godot
                opened, click on <c>Project &gt; Quit to Project List</c> (<c>Ctrl</c>
        <c />+<c />
        <c>Shift</c>
        <c />+<c />
        <c>Q</c>, <c>Ctrl</c>
        <c />+<c />
        <c>Option</c>
        <c />+<c />
        <c>Cmd</c>
        <c />+<c />
        <c>Q</c> on macOS)</p>
      <p>In the Project Manager, click the <c>Import</c> button to import the project.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Import Button</caption>
        <image source="instancing_import_button.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>The project manager is shown with the import button surrouned by a yellow box.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>In the pop-up that appears navigate to the folder you extracted.
                Double-click the <c>project.godot</c> file to open it.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Selecting Project to Import</caption>
        <image source="instancing_import_project_file.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>A project.godot file is selected in the file view of the project.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Finally, click the <c>Import</c> button.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Import Dialog</caption>
        <image source="instancing_import_and_edit_button.webp" width="50%">
         <shortdescription>The import dialog is shown with the edit now box checked.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>A window notifying you that the project was last opened in an older Godot version
                may appear. That's not an issue. Click <c>OK</c> to open the project.</p>
      <p>The project contains two packed scenes: <c>main.tscn</c>, containing walls against
                which the ball collides, and <c>ball.tscn</c>. The Main scene should open
                automatically. If you're seeing an empty 3D scene instead of the main scene, click the 2D button at the top of the screen.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>2D Button</caption>
        <image source="instancing_2d_scene_select.webp" width="40%">
         <shortdescription>The 2D button of the tabs is selected.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <figure>
        <caption>Main 2D Scene</caption>
        <image source="instancing_main_scene.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>The 2D scene is shown with walls in the shape of a mouth and a nose.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Let's add a ball as a child of the Main node. In the Scene dock, select the Main
                node. Then, click the link icon at the top of the scene dock. This button allows
                you to add an instance of a scene as a child of the currently selected node.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Link Button</caption>
        <image source="instancing_scene_link_button.webp" width="25%">
         <shortdescription>The link button in the scene dock is in a yellow box.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Double-click the ball scene to instance it.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Selecting The Instance</caption>
        <image source="instancing_instance_child_window.webp" width="60%">
         <shortdescription>The Ball.tscn file is selected.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>The ball appears in the top-left corner of the viewport.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Scene with new Ball Instance</caption>
        <image source="instancing_ball_instanced.webp" width="75%">
         <shortdescription>The main scene is shown with a ball above the left wall.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Click on it and drag it towards the center of the view.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>The Ball is Positioned</caption>
        <image source="instancing_ball_moved.webp" width="75%">
         <shortdescription>Just the 2D view is shown with the ball just to the left of the nose.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Play the game by pressing <c>F5</c> (<c>Cmd</c>
        <c />+<c />
        <c>B</c> on macOS). You should see it fall.</p>
      <p>Now, we want to create more instances of the Ball node. With the ball still
                selected, press <c>Ctrl</c>
        <c />+<c />
        <c>D</c> (<c>Cmd</c>
        <c />+<c />
        <c>D</c> on macOS) to call the duplicate
                command. Click and drag to move the new ball to a different location.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Two Balls after Duplication</caption>
        <image source="instancing_ball_duplicated.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>Just the 2D view is shown with 2 balls just to the left of the nose.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>You can repeat this process until you have several in the scene.</p>
      <figure xml:id="gs-instancing-six-balls-figure">
        <caption>Six Balls After Duplication</caption>
        <image source="instancing_main_scene_with_balls.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>Just the 2D view is shown with 6 balls to the rigtht and left of the nose.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Play the game again. You should now see every ball fall independently from one
                another. This is what instances do. Each is an independent reproduction of a
                template scene.</p>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-instancing-editing-scenes-and-instances">
      <title>Editing scenes and instances</title>
      <p>There is more to instances. With this feature, you can:</p>
      <p>
      <ol>
        <li>Change the properties of one ball without affecting the others using the
                        <c>Inspector</c>.</li>
        <li>Change the default properties of every Ball by opening the <c>ball.tscn</c> scene
                        and making a change to the Ball node there. Upon saving, all instances of the
                        Ball in the project will see their values update.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      <note>
        <title>Note</title>
        <p>Changing a property on an instance always overrides values from the
                    corresponding packed scene.</p>
        </note>
      <p>Let's try this. Double-click <c>ball.tscn</c> in the FileSystem to open it.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Selecting the Ball Scene</caption>
        <image source="instancing_ball_scene_open.webp" width="25%" >
         <shortdescription>The FileSystem dock is shown with the Ball.tscn file in a yellow box.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>In the Scene dock on the left, select the Ball node. Then, in the <c>Inspector</c> on the
                right, click on the <c>PhysicsMaterial</c> property to expand it.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Selecting Physics Material</caption>
        <image source="instancing_physics_material_expand.webp" width="30%">
         <shortdescription>The inspector dock is shown with the PhysicsMaterial Property in a yellow box.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Set its Bounce property to <c>0.5</c> by clicking on the number field, typing <c>0.5</c>,
                and pressing <c>Enter</c>.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Changing the Bounce Value</caption>
        <image source="instancing_property_bounce_updated.webp" width="27%">
         <shortdescription>The Bounce property is set to 0.5.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Play the game by pressing <c>F5</c> (<c>Cmd</c>
        <c />+<c />
        <c>B</c> on macOS) and notice how all balls now bounce a lot
                more. As the Ball scene is a template for all instances, modifying it and saving
                causes all instances to update accordingly.</p>
      <p>Let's now adjust an individual instance. Head back to the Main scene by clicking
                on the corresponding tab above the viewport.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Main Scene Tab</caption>
        <image source="instancing_scene_tabs.webp" width="25%">
         <shortdescription>The Ball tab is shown with the Main tab surrounded by a yellow box.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Select one of the instanced Ball nodes and, in the <c>Inspector</c>, set its
                <c>Gravity Scale</c> value to <c>10</c>.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Setting Gravity Scale</caption>
        <image source="instancing_property_gravity_scale.webp" width="30%">
         <shortdescription>The GravityScale is set to 10.0</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>A grey "revert" button appears next to the adjusted property.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Reverting Gravity Scale</caption>
        <image source="instancing_property_revert_icon.webp" width="30%">
         <shortdescription>The revert button has a yellow box around it.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>This icon indicates you are overriding a value from the source packed scene.
                Even if you modify the property in the original scene, the value override will
                be preserved in the instance. Clicking the revert icon will restore the
                property to the value in the saved scene.</p>
      <p>Rerun the game and notice how this ball now falls much faster than the others.</p>
      <note>
        <title>Note</title>
        <p>You may notice you are unable to change the values of the <c>PhysicsMaterial</c>
                    of the ball. This is because <c>PhysicsMaterial</c> is a <em>resource</em>, and needs
                    to be made unique before you can edit it in a scene that is linking to its
                    original scene. To make a resource unique for one instance, right-click on
                    the <c>Physics Material</c> property in the <c>Inspector</c> and click <c>Make Unique</c>
                    in the context menu.</p>
        <p>Resources are another essential building block of Godot games we will cover
                    in a later lesson.</p>
        </note>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-instancing-scene-instances-as-a-design-language">
      <title>Scene instances as a design language</title>
      <p>Instances and scenes in Godot offer an excellent design language, setting the
                engine apart from others out there. We designed Godot around this concept from
                the ground up.</p>
      <p>We recommend dismissing architectural code patterns when making games with
                Godot, such as Model-View-Controller (MVC) or Entity-Relationship diagrams.
                Instead, you can start by imagining the elements players will see in your game
                and structure your code around them.</p>
      <p>For example, you could break down a shooter game like so:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Entity Relationship Diagram for Shooter</caption>
        <image source="instancing_diagram_shooter.webp" width="75%">
         <shortdescription>A diagram with ten interconnected nodes.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>You can come up with a diagram like this for almost any type of game. Each
                rectangle represents an entity that's visible in the game from the player's
                perspective. The arrows point towards the instantiator of each scene.</p>
      <p>Once you have a diagram, we recommend creating a scene for each element listed
                in it to develop your game. You'll use instancing, either by code or directly in
                the editor, to build your tree of scenes.</p>
      <p>Programmers tend to spend a lot of time designing abstract architectures and
                trying to fit components into it. Designing based on scenes makes development
                faster and more straightforward, allowing you to focus on the game logic itself.
                Because most game components map directly to a scene, using a design based on
                scene instantiation means you need little other architectural code.</p>
      <p>Here's the example of a scene diagram for an open-world game with tons of assets
                and nested elements:</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Entity Relationship Diagram for Open-World Game</caption>
        <image source="instancing_diagram_open_world.webp" width="75%">
         <shortdescription>A diagram with fourteen interconnected nodes.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Imagine we started by creating the room. We could make a couple of different
                room scenes, with unique arrangements of furniture in them. Later, we could make
                a house scene that uses multiple room instances for the interior. We would
                create a citadel out of many instanced houses and a large terrain on which we
                would place the citadel. Each of these would be a scene instancing one or more sub-scenes.</p>
      <p>Later, we could create scenes representing guards and add them to the citadel.
                They would be indirectly added to the overall game world.</p>
      <p>With Godot, it's easy to iterate on your game like this, as all you need to do
                is create and instantiate more scenes. We designed the editor to be accessible
                to programmers, designers, and artists alike. A typical team development process
                can involve 2D or 3D artists, level designers, game designers, and animators,
                all working with the Godot editor.</p>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-instancing-summary">
      <title>Summary</title>
      <p>Instancing, the process of producing an object from a blueprint, has many handy
                uses. With scenes, it gives you:</p>
      <p>
      <ul>
        <li>The ability to divide your game into reusable components.</li>
        <li>A tool to structure and encapsulate complex systems.</li>
        <li>A language to think about your game project's structure in a natural way.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      </subsection>
    </section>
